--
-- A node is a task (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.cub)). Its name is an English sentence saying what it is;
-- the identifier it is declared under is how code refers to it. Its parts-of are the nodes it is a
-- child of: the reasons it exists, its value being exactly what it serves (there is no scalar).
-- A node is its statement: a proposition about the world. Its children are one route to it, never its
-- definition; other routes always exist, most unexpressed (Avik, 2026-11-16: "there are many open ways
-- I can kill this node. They are not all expressed. It can be killed while touching nothing that
-- apparently serves it. This is ubiquitously true"; "Quantum"; "The workflow needs logic. It is not
-- separate from the language"). A node is resolved when it has a witness: listed in `resolved` with the
-- evidence that its statement holds, achieved by any route, or every one of its children resolved.
-- Resolution is computed, the least set closed under that rule from the witnessed nodes; when a node
-- resolves, the routes beneath it that serve nothing unresolved are no longer open work. A node is
-- expanded into children only when its work begins, exactly when one of its children becomes in
-- progress. A node part of nothing is a root; the root of this work is what Avik wants. A node's
-- statement is the comment above its definition in workflow/hyperactivity.cub: what it is, why, and
-- what is known about it. A resolved node stays in the graph.
-- The one relation besides parts: `after`, pairs (n , ms): n's work cannot start until every node of
-- ms is resolved (waiting on a node is waiting on every leaf under it). The order of work is computed
-- from parts and after alone: every order is a topological sort of after; among work that can be
-- done in either order, X goes before Y when everything finishing Y first makes possible, finishing X
-- first also makes possible, and more (enabling is conjunctive: a node waiting on several becomes
-- possible only when all are resolved). lib/views.cub computes it (hyperactive order).
-- State: `in-progress`, the leaves being worked on now, and `resolved`, the nodes witnessed achieved;
-- every other open leaf is pending (there is one owner, Avik, and sessions hold no identity, so no node
-- says who works on it). The check requires in-progress to name leaves, no node to be both in progress
-- and resolved, and every expanded node to have a node in progress or resolved below it.
-- Essential context: `essential-context`, pairs (n , ds): documents (lib/document.cub: files of the
-- repository as objects of the code, a reference to a missing one failing the check) that are
-- essential context for working on n and on everything below it. The relation is deliberately loose:
-- "essential context", not "must be read before" (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.cub)).
data Node = node (name : List Word) (part-of : List Node)
